---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ElectricityCost from '@/components/tools/calc2/ElectricityCost.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="electricity-cost-calculator">
  <ElectricityCost client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the electricity cost calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The electricity cost calculator turns each appliance's power and hours of use into kilowatt-hours,
      the unit on your bill, and multiplies them by your price per kWh. It adds the appliances together
      for a day, the billing period you choose and a year of 365 days.
    </p>
    <pre><code>kWh per day = watts × hours per day × quantity ÷ 1,000
cost per day = kWh per day × price per kWh
cost for the period = cost per day × days
cost per year = cost per day × 365</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 1,500 W space heater used 3 hours a day uses 1,500 × 3 ÷ 1,000 = 4.5 kWh a day. Over 30 days that is
      135 kWh, which costs 135 × $0.15 = $20.25 at 15 cents per kWh, and $246.38 over a year.
    </p>

    <h2>The meter</h2>
    <p>
      Many US electromechanical meters turn their disc once for every 7.2 watt-hours; the figure is printed
      on the face as Kh 7.2. The disc speed is the load in watts divided by 7.2 × 3,600, so 1,000 W turns it
      138.9 times an hour, and 1,540 W once every 16.8 seconds. The drawing turns at that true rate for all
      the appliances you entered, running at the same time.
    </p>

    <h2>Typical power</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Appliance</th><th>Watts</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="tabular-nums">
        <tr><td>LED bulb</td><td>8 to 12</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Laptop</td><td>30 to 70</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Television</td><td>60 to 150</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Refrigerator, average while it cycles</td><td>100 to 200</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Microwave</td><td>800 to 1,200</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Space heater</td><td>1,500</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Clothes dryer</td><td>2,000 to 5,000</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Appliances that cycle, such as fridges, heaters with a thermostat and air conditioners, draw full power only part of the time. Use the hours they actually run, or measure them with a plug-in meter.</li>
      <li>Bills often add a fixed daily charge, taxes and time-of-use or tiered rates. Use the average price from your bill for a closer figure.</li>
      <li>Devices on standby still draw a few watts. Enter them with 24 hours a day.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
